    {
      "id": "-pc1E3d3G0gOuAMltQIvA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1578.3858720099142,
      "y": 49.445898123669394,
      "width": 989.9999999999999,
      "height": 3249.9999999999995,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ak",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1598537113,
      "version": 100,
      "versionNonce": 197755001,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1764542363697,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n---\n\n# ⭐ **Concept 1: Node.js Event Loop & Single-Threaded Architecture**\n\n---\n\n# ❓ What does “single-threaded” actually mean?\n\nNode.js uses **one thread** to execute your JavaScript code.\n\nThat means:\n\n### ✔ Only ONE piece of JS can execute at a time\n\n### ✔ Only ONE route handler can run at a time\n\n### ✔ Only ONE CPU-heavy task can run at a time\n\nThis is unlike languages like Java, Go, or Python, which can run many threads in parallel.\n\nNode's single thread is called:\n\n### → **The Event Loop**\n\n---\n\n# ❓ How does the Event Loop normally work?\n\nImagine Node as a **single cashier** in a store.\n\nCustomers (requests) stand in line:\n\n* “GET /”\n* “GET /fast”\n* “GET /about”\n\nNode processes them quickly one by one:\n\n* If the job is small → the next customer moves quickly\n* If the job is long → everyone waits\n\nExpress routes are like tasks in this line.\n\n---\n\n# ❗ The BIG Problem: CPU-heavy tasks BLOCK the event loop\n\nLook at this function in your `app.js`:\n\n```js\nfunction doWork() {\n    for (let i = 0; i < 1e9; i++) {\n    }\n    return \"Work Done\";\n}\n```\n\nThis loop runs **1 BILLION** times.\n\nBecause Node is single-threaded:\n\n➡️ **While this loop runs, NO OTHER REQUEST CAN RUN.**\n➡️ **Your whole server becomes unresponsive.**\n➡️ **Other users will wait… possibly for seconds or minutes.**\n\nThis is called:\n\n### ❗ **BLOCKING The Event Loop**\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 Why does a loop block the server?\n\nBecause JavaScript code runs on the **main thread**, and until a function finishes:\n\n* Node cannot move to next request\n* It cannot run timers\n* It cannot run other Express routes\n* It cannot respond to clients\n\nNode is stuck.\n\nSo if someone calls the `/` route that runs `doWork()`:\n\n➡️ **Every other user sees the server frozen.**\n\nEven `/fast` won’t work.\nEven `console.log` may delay.\n\n---\n\n# ✔ Why doesn’t async code block like this?\n\nAsync functions like:\n\n* setTimeout\n* reading files\n* database calls\n* HTTP requests\n\ndo NOT block the event loop.\n\nBecause Node offloads them to internal threads (libuv) or OS APIs.\n\nSo:\n\n```js\napp.get('/', async (req, res) => {\n   await fetch(\"https://...\")  // non-blocking\n   res.send(\"done\");\n})\n```\n\n**This does NOT stop Node.**\n\nBut CPU-heavy code written in JavaScript DOES.\n\n---\n\n# 🎉 Summary of Concept 1 (Event Loop & Blocking)\n\n1. Node runs JavaScript in **a single thread**.\n2. Heavy tasks (big loops, calculations, string building) **freeze the server**.\n3. While a heavy task runs, **no request is handled**.\n4. This is why your normal `/` route blocks everything.\n5. To avoid blocking, we need child processes, worker threads, or cluster.\n\n---\n",
      "fontSize": 19.999999999999993,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n---\n\n# ⭐ **Concept 1: Node.js Event Loop & Single-Threaded Architecture**\n\n---\n\n# ❓ What does “single-threaded” actually mean?\n\nNode.js uses **one thread** to execute your JavaScript code.\n\nThat means:\n\n### ✔ Only ONE piece of JS can execute at a time\n\n### ✔ Only ONE route handler can run at a time\n\n### ✔ Only ONE CPU-heavy task can run at a time\n\nThis is unlike languages like Java, Go, or Python, which can run many threads in parallel.\n\nNode's single thread is called:\n\n### → **The Event Loop**\n\n---\n\n# ❓ How does the Event Loop normally work?\n\nImagine Node as a **single cashier** in a store.\n\nCustomers (requests) stand in line:\n\n* “GET /”\n* “GET /fast”\n* “GET /about”\n\nNode processes them quickly one by one:\n\n* If the job is small → the next customer moves quickly\n* If the job is long → everyone waits\n\nExpress routes are like tasks in this line.\n\n---\n\n# ❗ The BIG Problem: CPU-heavy tasks BLOCK the event loop\n\nLook at this function in your `app.js`:\n\n```js\nfunction doWork() {\n    for (let i = 0; i < 1e9; i++) {\n    }\n    return \"Work Done\";\n}\n```\n\nThis loop runs **1 BILLION** times.\n\nBecause Node is single-threaded:\n\n➡️ **While this loop runs, NO OTHER REQUEST CAN RUN.**\n➡️ **Your whole server becomes unresponsive.**\n➡️ **Other users will wait… possibly for seconds or minutes.**\n\nThis is called:\n\n### ❗ **BLOCKING The Event Loop**\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 Why does a loop block the server?\n\nBecause JavaScript code runs on the **main thread**, and until a function finishes:\n\n* Node cannot move to next request\n* It cannot run timers\n* It cannot run other Express routes\n* It cannot respond to clients\n\nNode is stuck.\n\nSo if someone calls the `/` route that runs `doWork()`:\n\n➡️ **Every other user sees the server frozen.**\n\nEven `/fast` won’t work.\nEven `console.log` may delay.\n\n---\n\n# ✔ Why doesn’t async code block like this?\n\nAsync functions like:\n\n* setTimeout\n* reading files\n* database calls\n* HTTP requests\n\ndo NOT block the event loop.\n\nBecause Node offloads them to internal threads (libuv) or OS APIs.\n\nSo:\n\n```js\napp.get('/', async (req, res) => {\n   await fetch(\"https://...\")  // non-blocking\n   res.send(\"done\");\n})\n```\n\n**This does NOT stop Node.**\n\nBut CPU-heavy code written in JavaScript DOES.\n\n---\n\n# 🎉 Summary of Concept 1 (Event Loop & Blocking)\n\n1. Node runs JavaScript in **a single thread**.\n2. Heavy tasks (big loops, calculations, string building) **freeze the server**.\n3. While a heavy task runs, **no request is handled**.\n4. This is why your normal `/` route blocks everything.\n5. To avoid blocking, we need child processes, worker threads, or cluster.\n\n---\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
