      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "---\n\n# ⭐ **Concept 2: Child Processes (exec, spawn, fork)**\n\nThis is the KEY concept for understanding how your `/fast` route avoids blocking.\n\n---\n\n# 🔥 Why do we need child processes?\n\nWe learned that heavy CPU work blocks Node’s single thread.\nSo we need a way to run heavy code **outside** the main server.\n\nNode gives us a solution:\n\n### ✔ Create another Node process\n\n### ✔ Run the heavy code inside that separate process\n\n### ✔ Keep the main Express server free and fast\n\nThis is exactly what child processes do.\n\n---\n\n# 🧩 What is a child process?\n\nA child process is like opening another terminal window and manually typing:\n\n```\nnode heavyWork.js\n```\n\nNode does it programmatically for you.\n\nSo now you have:\n\n### ✔ Main Node process — running Express\n\n### ✔ Child Node process — running heavyWork.js\n\nThe child process runs completely independently.\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 Why is this good?\n\nBecause each child process:\n\n* has its own CPU time\n* has its own event loop\n* has its own memory\n* does NOT block the main Node server\n\nWhile the child is busy with a heavy loop:\n\n➡️ The Express server continues responding to users.\n➡️ No freezing.\n➡️ No blocking.\n\n---\n\n# ✨ 3 Ways to create child processes in Node:\n\nNode provides:\n\n### 1️⃣ **exec()**\n\n* Runs a command\n* Waits for the command to finish\n* Returns **entire output in a single string (buffered)**\n  Good for small/medium output.\n\n### 2️⃣ **spawn()**\n\n* Runs a command\n* Returns output **in chunks (streaming)**\n  Good for huge output or real-time data.\n\n### 3️⃣ **fork()**\n\n* Used for Node.js modules only\n* Optimized for communication between parent and child\n\n---\n\n# ✔ You used `exec()` in your code:\n\n```js\nexec(\"node heavyWork.js\", (error, stdout, stderr) => {\n    res.send(stdout);\n})\n```\n\nThis does:\n\n### ➜ Start a new process that runs `heavyWork.js`\n\n### ➜ Waits for heavyWork.js to finish\n\n### ➜ Collects everything the child printed\n\n### ➜ Sends the output back to the client\n\nMeanwhile…\n\nYour Express server is COMPLETELY FREE to handle other requests.\nNo blocking at all.\n\n---\n\n# 🔍 How does heavyWork.js return data?\n\nLook at this line:\n\n```js\nprocess.stdout.write(output);\n```\n\nWhen you use exec/spawn, the child’s **stdout** becomes the data you get in:\n\n```js\nstdout\n```\n\nSo your child process prints the result,\nand your main process receives it.\n\n---\n\n# 🆚 exec() vs spawn() (beginner-friendly)\n\n### **exec()**\n\n* Buffers entire output in memory\n* Simple callback\n* Good for commands that finish quickly\n\n### **spawn()**\n\n* Gives data as it is generated\n* Good for huge outputs\n* Good for long-running tasks\n\nIn your project, exec is enough because:\n\n* The output is big but not gigantic\n* The process ends fairly quickly\n\n---\n\n# 📌 Summary of Concept 2 (Child Processes)\n\n1. Node can create new processes to avoid blocking the main thread.\n2. `exec()` runs a command and gives all output at once.\n3. `spawn()` streams output chunk-by-chunk.\n4. `fork()` is for communication-heavy Node child scripts.\n5. Your `/fast` route uses exec to run heavyWork.js in a separate process, keeping the server fast.\n\n---\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "---\n\n# ⭐ **Concept 2: Child Processes (exec, spawn, fork)**\n\nThis is the KEY concept for understanding how your `/fast` route avoids blocking.\n\n---\n\n# 🔥 Why do we need child processes?\n\nWe learned that heavy CPU work blocks Node’s single thread.\nSo we need a way to run heavy code **outside** the main server.\n\nNode gives us a solution:\n\n### ✔ Create another Node process\n\n### ✔ Run the heavy code inside that separate process\n\n### ✔ Keep the main Express server free and fast\n\nThis is exactly what child processes do.\n\n---\n\n# 🧩 What is a child process?\n\nA child process is like opening another terminal window and manually typing:\n\n```\nnode heavyWork.js\n```\n\nNode does it programmatically for you.\n\nSo now you have:\n\n### ✔ Main Node process — running Express\n\n### ✔ Child Node process — running heavyWork.js\n\nThe child process runs completely independently.\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 Why is this good?\n\nBecause each child process:\n\n* has its own CPU time\n* has its own event loop\n* has its own memory\n* does NOT block the main Node server\n\nWhile the child is busy with a heavy loop:\n\n➡️ The Express server continues responding to users.\n➡️ No freezing.\n➡️ No blocking.\n\n---\n\n# ✨ 3 Ways to create child processes in Node:\n\nNode provides:\n\n### 1️⃣ **exec()**\n\n* Runs a command\n* Waits for the command to finish\n* Returns **entire output in a single string (buffered)**\n  Good for small/medium output.\n\n### 2️⃣ **spawn()**\n\n* Runs a command\n* Returns output **in chunks (streaming)**\n  Good for huge output or real-time data.\n\n### 3️⃣ **fork()**\n\n* Used for Node.js modules only\n* Optimized for communication between parent and child\n\n---\n\n# ✔ You used `exec()` in your code:\n\n```js\nexec(\"node heavyWork.js\", (error, stdout, stderr) => {\n    res.send(stdout);\n})\n```\n\nThis does:\n\n### ➜ Start a new process that runs `heavyWork.js`\n\n### ➜ Waits for heavyWork.js to finish\n\n### ➜ Collects everything the child printed\n\n### ➜ Sends the output back to the client\n\nMeanwhile…\n\nYour Express server is COMPLETELY FREE to handle other requests.\nNo blocking at all.\n\n---\n\n# 🔍 How does heavyWork.js return data?\n\nLook at this line:\n\n```js\nprocess.stdout.write(output);\n```\n\nWhen you use exec/spawn, the child’s **stdout** becomes the data you get in:\n\n```js\nstdout\n```\n\nSo your child process prints the result,\nand your main process receives it.\n\n---\n\n# 🆚 exec() vs spawn() (beginner-friendly)\n\n### **exec()**\n\n* Buffers entire output in memory\n* Simple callback\n* Good for commands that finish quickly\n\n### **spawn()**\n\n* Gives data as it is generated\n* Good for huge outputs\n* Good for long-running tasks\n\nIn your project, exec is enough because:\n\n* The output is big but not gigantic\n* The process ends fairly quickly\n\n---\n\n# 📌 Summary of Concept 2 (Child Processes)\n\n1. Node can create new processes to avoid blocking the main thread.\n2. `exec()` runs a command and gives all output at once.\n3. `spawn()` streams output chunk-by-chunk.\n4. `fork()` is for communication-heavy Node child scripts.\n5. Your `/fast` route uses exec to run heavyWork.js in a separate process, keeping the server fast.\n\n---\n\n",
