      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1764542521979,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "---\n\n# ⭐ **Concept 3: Node.js Clustering**\n\nChild processes (exec/spawn/fork) help you **offload heavy work**,\nbut **clustering** helps you scale your entire server to use all CPU cores.\n\nLet's break it down simply.\n\n---\n\n# ❓ Why do we need clustering?\n\nEven if you write perfect non-blocking Express code…\n\n➡️ Your Node app still runs on **ONE CPU core**\n➡️ Even if your machine has **8 cores**, Node uses only **1**\n\nSo Node can handle only as many requests as a single CPU allows.\n\n### ❗ Clustering solves this.\n\n---\n\n# ⭐ What does clustering do?\n\nClustering creates:\n\n### ✔ **1 Master process**\n\n(“manager” process)\n\n### ✔ **N Worker processes**\n\n(one per CPU core)\n\nEach worker is a **full copy of your server**.\n\nWhen a request comes:\n\n➡️ Master distributes the incoming requests equally\n➡️ Workers handle them independently\n\nSo if you have 8 cores → You get **8 servers running in parallel**.\n\n---\n\n# 📌 In your code (cluster-app.js)\n\n### 🎩 **The master process**\n\n```js\nif (cluster.isPrimary) {\n    for (let i = 0; i < numCPUs; i++) {\n        cluster.fork();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis means:\n\n* Detect how many CPU cores you have (say 8)\n* Start 8 worker processes\n* Each worker runs your Express app\n\n---\n\n### 🧑‍🏭 **The worker processes**\n\n```js\nelse {\n    const express = require('express');\n    const app = express();\n    app.get('/', (req, res) => {\n        res.send(`Hello ${process.pid}`);\n    });\n}\n```\n\nEach worker:\n\n* is a completely separate Node process\n* runs its own Express server\n* listens on port 4444\n* has its own PID (process ID)\n\nWhen you refresh the page, you may see different PIDs because different workers respond.\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 Why is clustering useful?\n\nIt lets you serve WAY more users.\n\n| Setup                  | Requests you can handle |\n| ---------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| 1 Node process         | limited by 1 CPU        |\n| Cluster with 4 workers | roughly 4x the traffic  |\n| Cluster with 8 workers | roughly 8x the traffic  |\n\nClustering uses **all CPU cores** efficiently.\n\n---\n\n# 🧩 Clustering does NOT solve CPU-heavy code by itself\n\nThis is important:\n\nEven with clustering,\nif a worker gets a heavy request (like running a huge loop):\n\n➡️ That worker becomes blocked\n➡️ But other workers continue working\n➡️ So clustering improves reliability but not per-worker performance\n\nThis is why **child processes + clustering** is the ideal combo.\n\n---\n\n# 📌 Summary of Concept 3 (Clustering)\n\n1. Node normally uses only 1 CPU core.\n2. Clustering starts multiple copies of your server (one per CPU).\n3. Master distributes incoming requests to worker processes.\n4. If one worker is busy or crashes, others still work.\n5. Clustering increases performance but does not fix heavy CPU blocking inside a worker.\n\n---\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "---\n\n# ⭐ **Concept 3: Node.js Clustering**\n\nChild processes (exec/spawn/fork) help you **offload heavy work**,\nbut **clustering** helps you scale your entire server to use all CPU cores.\n\nLet's break it down simply.\n\n---\n\n# ❓ Why do we need clustering?\n\nEven if you write perfect non-blocking Express code…\n\n➡️ Your Node app still runs on **ONE CPU core**\n➡️ Even if your machine has **8 cores**, Node uses only **1**\n\nSo Node can handle only as many requests as a single CPU allows.\n\n### ❗ Clustering solves this.\n\n---\n\n# ⭐ What does clustering do?\n\nClustering creates:\n\n### ✔ **1 Master process**\n\n(“manager” process)\n\n### ✔ **N Worker processes**\n\n(one per CPU core)\n\nEach worker is a **full copy of your server**.\n\nWhen a request comes:\n\n➡️ Master distributes the incoming requests equally\n➡️ Workers handle them independently\n\nSo if you have 8 cores → You get **8 servers running in parallel**.\n\n---\n\n# 📌 In your code (cluster-app.js)\n\n### 🎩 **The master process**\n\n```js\nif (cluster.isPrimary) {\n    for (let i = 0; i < numCPUs; i++) {\n        cluster.fork();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis means:\n\n* Detect how many CPU cores you have (say 8)\n* Start 8 worker processes\n* Each worker runs your Express app\n\n---\n\n### 🧑‍🏭 **The worker processes**\n\n```js\nelse {\n    const express = require('express');\n    const app = express();\n    app.get('/', (req, res) => {\n        res.send(`Hello ${process.pid}`);\n    });\n}\n```\n\nEach worker:\n\n* is a completely separate Node process\n* runs its own Express server\n* listens on port 4444\n* has its own PID (process ID)\n\nWhen you refresh the page, you may see different PIDs because different workers respond.\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 Why is clustering useful?\n\nIt lets you serve WAY more users.\n\n| Setup                  | Requests you can handle |\n| ---------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| 1 Node process         | limited by 1 CPU        |\n| Cluster with 4 workers | roughly 4x the traffic  |\n| Cluster with 8 workers | roughly 8x the traffic  |\n\nClustering uses **all CPU cores** efficiently.\n\n---\n\n# 🧩 Clustering does NOT solve CPU-heavy code by itself\n\nThis is important:\n\nEven with clustering,\nif a worker gets a heavy request (like running a huge loop):\n\n➡️ That worker becomes blocked\n➡️ But other workers continue working\n➡️ So clustering improves reliability but not per-worker performance\n\nThis is why **child processes + clustering** is the ideal combo.\n\n---\n\n# 📌 Summary of Concept 3 (Clustering)\n\n1. Node normally uses only 1 CPU core.\n2. Clustering starts multiple copies of your server (one per CPU).\n3. Master distributes incoming requests to worker processes.\n4. If one worker is busy or crashes, others still work.\n5. Clustering increases performance but does not fix heavy CPU blocking inside a worker.\n\n---\n\n",
