      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "---\n\n# ⭐ **Concept 4: How Child Processes Communicate (IPC & stdout)**\n\nWhen you run a child process using `exec()` or `spawn()`,\nyour main Node process and the child process need a way to talk.\n\nNode uses a simple communication system:\n\n### ✔ The child process **prints**\n\n### ✔ The parent process **receives what was printed**\n\n### ✔ The parent process sends that data to Express → to the user\n\nThe “print-and-receive” channel is called:\n\n# ➤ **stdout (standard output)**\n\n# ➤ **stderr (standard error)**\n\nThese are the same output channels used in a terminal.\n\n---\n\n# 🔥 Example using your heavyWork.js\n\nInside heavyWork.js:\n\n```js\nprocess.stdout.write(output);\n```\n\nThis line sends the large string to the **stdout** channel of the child process.\n\nNormally stdout appears in the console…\nBut when we run it with `exec()`, Node intercepts it.\n\nSo inside `exec()`, you get:\n\n```js\nexec(\"node heavyWork.js\", (error, stdout, stderr) => {\n    res.send(stdout);\n});\n```\n\n* `stdout` → contains whatever child printed using `process.stdout.write()`\n* `stderr` → contains error messages\n* `error` → contains runtime errors (if any)\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 Why is stdout important?\n\nBecause this is **how child processes return data** back to your main Express server.\n\nChild process cannot do:\n\n❌ `return output` → parent won’t get it\n❌ `res.send()` → child has no access to Express\n\nInstead, it must:\n\n### ✔ Print to stdout\n\n### ✔ Parent receives it as a string\n\n### ✔ Parent sends it to the browser\n\nThis is literally how Linux processes communicate.\n\n---\n\n# 🧩 What about spawn()?\n\nSpawn streams the data in pieces:\n\n```js\nchild.stdout.on('data', chunk => {\n    console.log(chunk.toString());\n});\n```\n\nThis is useful for:\n\n* large outputs\n* real-time logs\n* progress updates\n\nexec gives everything at once\nspawn gives chunks continuously\n\n---\n\n# 🌟 Real-world analogy\n\nThink of child process communication like this:\n\n* Child process writes messages onto a conveyor belt (stdout).\n* Parent process is waiting at the end of the belt.\n* Whatever arrives, the parent reads.\n* Parent wraps it and sends it to the client.\n\nSimple, reliable, universal.\n\n---\n\n# 📌 Summary of Concept 4 (IPC & stdout)\n\n1. Child processes send results by **printing to stdout**.\n2. `exec()` captures everything and returns it in `stdout`.\n3. `spawn()` streams data chunk-by-chunk from stdout.\n4. stderr captures error output from the child.\n5. This is how heavyWork.js communicates with your main server.\n\n---\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "---\n\n# ⭐ **Concept 4: How Child Processes Communicate (IPC & stdout)**\n\nWhen you run a child process using `exec()` or `spawn()`,\nyour main Node process and the child process need a way to talk.\n\nNode uses a simple communication system:\n\n### ✔ The child process **prints**\n\n### ✔ The parent process **receives what was printed**\n\n### ✔ The parent process sends that data to Express → to the user\n\nThe “print-and-receive” channel is called:\n\n# ➤ **stdout (standard output)**\n\n# ➤ **stderr (standard error)**\n\nThese are the same output channels used in a terminal.\n\n---\n\n# 🔥 Example using your heavyWork.js\n\nInside heavyWork.js:\n\n```js\nprocess.stdout.write(output);\n```\n\nThis line sends the large string to the **stdout** channel of the child process.\n\nNormally stdout appears in the console…\nBut when we run it with `exec()`, Node intercepts it.\n\nSo inside `exec()`, you get:\n\n```js\nexec(\"node heavyWork.js\", (error, stdout, stderr) => {\n    res.send(stdout);\n});\n```\n\n* `stdout` → contains whatever child printed using `process.stdout.write()`\n* `stderr` → contains error messages\n* `error` → contains runtime errors (if any)\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 Why is stdout important?\n\nBecause this is **how child processes return data** back to your main Express server.\n\nChild process cannot do:\n\n❌ `return output` → parent won’t get it\n❌ `res.send()` → child has no access to Express\n\nInstead, it must:\n\n### ✔ Print to stdout\n\n### ✔ Parent receives it as a string\n\n### ✔ Parent sends it to the browser\n\nThis is literally how Linux processes communicate.\n\n---\n\n# 🧩 What about spawn()?\n\nSpawn streams the data in pieces:\n\n```js\nchild.stdout.on('data', chunk => {\n    console.log(chunk.toString());\n});\n```\n\nThis is useful for:\n\n* large outputs\n* real-time logs\n* progress updates\n\nexec gives everything at once\nspawn gives chunks continuously\n\n---\n\n# 🌟 Real-world analogy\n\nThink of child process communication like this:\n\n* Child process writes messages onto a conveyor belt (stdout).\n* Parent process is waiting at the end of the belt.\n* Whatever arrives, the parent reads.\n* Parent wraps it and sends it to the client.\n\nSimple, reliable, universal.\n\n---\n\n# 📌 Summary of Concept 4 (IPC & stdout)\n\n1. Child processes send results by **printing to stdout**.\n2. `exec()` captures everything and returns it in `stdout`.\n3. `spawn()` streams data chunk-by-chunk from stdout.\n4. stderr captures error output from the child.\n5. This is how heavyWork.js communicates with your main server.\n\n---\n",
