      "originalText": "---\n\n# ⭐ **THE FULL INTERNAL WORKING OF NODE.JS — STEP BY STEP**\n\nWe will walk through exactly what happens *inside Node* when your code runs.\n\n---\n\n# 🔥 PART 1 — The Core Problem Node Solves\n\n**JavaScript is traditionally single-threaded.**\n\nOriginally, JavaScript was created for browsers:\n\n* No threads\n* No parallelism\n* Only one execution flow\n\nBUT modern applications need:\n\n* file reading\n* network communication\n* timers\n* DNS lookups\n* cryptography\n* compression\n* databases\n\nIf JS handled all of this on a single thread, it would freeze constantly.\n\n---\n\n# ⭐ PART 2 — Node.js is built from **two major components**\n\n### 🧠 **1. V8 (JavaScript Engine)**\n\n* Executes your JS code (functions, loops, variables, Express handlers)\n* This is single-threaded\n* This part can get blocked if you run CPU-heavy JS\n\n### 🧠 **2. libuv (C/C++ Library)**\n\nThis is the MOST IMPORTANT part of Node’s architecture.\nIt provides:\n\n* The **Event Loop**\n* The **Thread Pool**\n* The **Event Demultiplexer**\n* Async File I/O\n* Async Network I/O\n* TCP/UDP implementation\n* Timers\n\nlibuv is what gives Node.js its **asynchronous, non-blocking** behavior.\n\n---\n\n# ⭐ PART 3 — **Event Demultiplexer (epoll, kqueue, IOCP)**\n\nThis is what waits for actual OS-level events.\n\nOperating systems provide APIs that allow programs to wait for events:\n\n* Linux → **epoll**\n* macOS → **kqueue**\n* Windows → **IOCP (IO Completion Ports)**\n\n### What does the demultiplexer do?\n\nLet’s say you run this:\n\n```js\nfs.readFile(\"a.txt\", callback);\n```\n\nThe demultiplexer (via the OS) waits for:\n\n* “File read completed”\n\nWhen the event occurs, the demultiplexer tells **libuv**,\nand libuv tells the **Event Loop**:\n\n➡️ “Hey, this callback is ready to run!”\n\nThe event loop then schedules the callback to run inside V8.\n\n### IMPORTANT:\n\nDemultiplexer only manages **events provided by OS natively**, such as:\n\n* Network I/O\n* Timers\n* Some async OS file operations\n\nFor things the OS cannot do asynchronously (like crypto), Node uses the thread pool.\n\n---\n\n# ⭐ PART 4 — **Thread Pool (4 threads by default)**\n\nSome tasks CANNOT be done asynchronously at the OS level.\n\nExample:\n\n* reading a file in some OSes\n* crypto operations\n* compression\n* DNS resolution\n\nSo Node sends these tasks to four background threads in **libuv’s thread pool**.\n\nThese are **not JavaScript threads**.\nThey are C++ threads in libuv.\n\n### Example:\n\n```js\nfs.readFile(\"a.txt\", callback);\n```\n\nIf the OS cannot do async file I/O directly, libuv will:\n\n1. Put the “read this file” job into its thread pool\n2. A background C++ thread will read the file\n3. When finished, the thread sends the result back to the event demultiplexer\n4. Event Loop receives the notification\n5. Callback is executed in the JS thread\n\n### So the thread pool handles:\n\n✔ CPU-heavy async work\n✔ I/O that OS can’t do asynchronously\n✔ Crypto\n✔ Compression\n✔ DNS\n\nThis is why async code does NOT block Node’s event loop.\n\n---\n\n# ⭐ PART 5 — **Event Loop: The Heart of Node**\n\nThe event loop takes tasks from libuv and runs the callbacks inside V8 (JavaScript).\n\nIt has **6 phases**, each doing a specific job.\n\nLet’s break them down in plain English:\n\n---\n\n## **PHASE 1 — TIMERS**\n\nRuns callbacks scheduled by `setTimeout()` and `setInterval()`.\n\nNot EXACTLY at the specified time.\nNode checks:\n\n* “Is the timer expired?”\n* If yes → run the callback.\n\n---\n\n## **PHASE 2 — PENDING CALLBACKS**\n\nRuns I/O callbacks that were deferred by the OS or libuv for some reason.\n\nMostly low-level stuff.\n\n---\n\n## **PHASE 3 — IDLE / PREPARE**\n\nInternal to libuv — not relevant to daily coding.\n\n---\n\n## **PHASE 4 — POLL (THE MOST IMPORTANT PHASE)**\n\n### The Poll phase does two major things:\n\n### 1️⃣  Check if there are I/O events ready\n\nExamples:\n\n* File reading is done\n* Network packet arrived\n* DNS lookup finished\n\nIf there are → run the callbacks.\n\n### 2️⃣  If nothing is ready, poll BLOCKS\n\nIt waits until:\n\n* new I/O task is ready\n* or a timer is about to expire\n* or setImmediate callbacks need to run\n\nThis is how Node stays efficient without using 100% CPU.\n\n---\n\n## **PHASE 5 — CHECK**\n\nRuns callbacks scheduled by `setImmediate()`.\n\n---\n\n## **PHASE 6 — CLOSE CALLBACKS**\n\nRuns close event handlers like:\n\n```js\nsocket.on('close', ...)\n```\n\n---\n\n# ⭐ PART 6 — How ALL OF THIS behaves in YOUR CODE\n\nLet’s connect all internal pieces to your project.\n\n---\n\n# 💥 **Case 1: CPU-heavy JS loop (doWork())**\n\n```js\nfor (let i = 0; i < 1e9; i++) {\n}\n```\n\nThis runs inside **V8**, inside the **main JS thread**.\n\n### What happens internally?\n\n* Event Loop CANNOT run\n* libuv cannot schedule callbacks\n* Other requests cannot be processed\n* The server is frozen\n* Even timers won’t run\n\n### Why?\n\nBecause V8 is BLOCKED, and nothing else can execute until this loop finishes.\n\n---\n\n# 💥 **Case 2: exec(\"node heavyWork.js\")**\n\nWhen you run:\n\n```js\nexec(\"node heavyWork.js\", ...)\n```\n\nNode forks a NEW OS process.\n\nThis new process:\n\n* has its **own V8 engine**\n* has its **own libuv**\n* has its **own event loop**\n* has its **own thread pool**\n\nIt runs completely separately from your main server.\n\nYour main server remains free because:\n\n➡️ The heavy loop is NOT running in its event loop\n➡️ The child process prints to stdout\n➡️ Parent collects the output and responds\n\n---\n\n# 💥 **Case 3: Clustering**\n\nClustering creates:\n\n* 1 master process\n* N worker processes (each with its own V8 + libuv)\n\nEach worker handles some requests.\n\nIf one worker is blocked due to CPU-heavy work:\n\n* The other workers keep running\n* The server stays partially alive\n\n---\n\n# ⭐ FINAL UNDERSTANDING\n\nHere is the **actual internal pipeline** when your code runs:\n\n```\nJS code runs → V8 thread blocks or runs callbacks\n     |\n     V\nAsync operations → libuv receives them\n     |\n     V\nCan OS handle async?\n     |\n    Yes → Event Demultiplexer waits → Event Loop executes callback\n    No  → Thread Pool runs task → Event Demultiplexer → Event Loop → callback\n```\n\n---\n\n# ⭐ Ultimate Summary (TRUE internal understanding)\n\n1. **V8** executes JS and is single-threaded → CPU tasks block it.\n2. **libuv** gives Node async capabilities.\n3. **Event Demultiplexer** (epoll/kqueue/IOCP) waits for OS events.\n4. **Thread Pool** handles expensive async operations in background threads.\n5. **Event Loop** schedules callbacks when async work completes.\n6. **child_process** and **cluster** create entirely separate Node processes, each with their own V8 + libuv.\n\nThis is EXACTLY why your `/fast` route works and your `/` route freezes.\n\n---\n",
